(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-dermatology-pathophys-quiz"]={"title":"Dermatology Pathophysiology Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the cell population of the epidermis?","choices":["Keratinocytes, melanocytes, Merkel cells and Langerhans cells, with keratinocytes about 90 percent","Fibroblasts, macrophages, adipocytes and mast cells, with fibroblasts predominating","Melanocytes, Merkel cells, fibroblasts and histiocytes, with melanocytes predominating","Keratinocytes and melanocytes only, in roughly equal proportion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The epidermis holds keratinocytes, melanocytes, Merkel cells and Langerhans cells, and keratinocytes, which produce keratin, make up about 90 percent of it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A nodule is greater than 5 millimeters. What tissue does it arise from?","choices":["Superficial epidermis only, with no dermal involvement","Fluid accumulating between epidermis and dermis","Dilated superficial dermal capillaries with no inflammatory infiltration at all","Dermal-based tissue with dense collagen bundles, extending into subcutaneous tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nodule is an elevated, solid lesion over 5 millimeters: a dermal-based lesion of dense collagen bundles extending into subcutaneous tissue. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which description of Henoch-Schonlein purpura is accurate?","choices":["The skin alone, with no systemic involvement whatsoever, at any age at all","Skin, joints, kidneys and intestines, commonly in children aged 3 to 10 years","Skin and lungs only, commonly in older adults","Skin and liver, commonly in neonates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — an immunoglobulin A vasculitis, which is why it is not confined to the skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How do hypertrophic scars and keloids depart from normal healing?","choices":["Keratinocyte division slows and elastin degrades, thinning the tissue","The inflammatory phase never resolves, so granulation tissue never forms at all and the wound remains open indefinitely without ever progressing to the proliferative or remodeling phases that normally follow","Type I collagen is replaced by weaker type III in basket-weave orientation","Fibroblast dysregulation prolongs the proliferative phase, collagen deposition and degradation become imbalanced during remodeling, and collagen bundles develop haphazardly and exceed the boundaries of the initial wound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two phases go wrong, and exceeding the original wound margin is the defining outcome. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which description of leukonychia is accurate?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue in the nail bed","Increased capillary density with release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the nail bed tissue itself","Temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed, usually harmless nail bed damage but also seen with heavy metal poisoning","A halt of keratin production, associated with illness and malnourishment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leukonychia, or Mee's lines, shows white streaks or spots from temporary incomplete keratinization at the nail bed, usually from harmless nail bed damage. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which description of the inflammatory phase of wound healing is accurate?","choices":["Days 1 to 3: type I collagen is laid down in basket-weave orientation","Days 1 to 3: a fibrin hemostatic plug forms, neutrophils and macrophages remove dead tissue, and growth factors and cytokines are secreted to signal the next phase","Days 4 to 21: granulation tissue forms, comprised of macrophages, fibroblasts and endothelial cells that rebuild the wound bed from below over three weeks","Days 21 to one year: type III collagen is replaced by type I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The phase both cleans the wound and issues the signal that starts proliferation. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What distinguishes a vesicle from a bulla?","choices":["A vesicle is up to 5 millimeters with fluid containing inflammatory cells collecting within or directly beneath the epidermis; a bulla is greater than 5 millimeters and involves separation of epidermis from dermis","A vesicle is greater than 5 millimeters and a bulla is up to 5 millimeters in diameter, and the two share an identical mechanism of fluid accumulation entirely within the epidermis rather than beneath it","A vesicle is solid and a bulla is fluid-filled","A vesicle contains purulent material and a bulla contains serous fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The bulla represents a deeper plane of cleavage, not merely a bigger blister. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Eczematous dermatitis results from which type of inflammatory reaction?","choices":["T cell-mediated","Immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation alone","Neutrophil-driven suppurative inflammation","Complement-mediated vessel destruction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Eczematous dermatitis typically results from T cell-mediated inflammatory reactions, with insufficient filaggrin compromising the epidermal barrier. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which dermal cell mediates immunoglobulin E-mediated inflammation?","choices":["The mast cell","The fibroblast","The melanocyte","The keratinocyte"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its degranulation is the mechanism behind both the wheal and urticaria. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How are dermatofibroma and lipoma characterized in the tissue?","choices":["Dermatofibroma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes; lipoma is a fibrous tissue overgrowth within the dermis","Both are malignant proliferations of keratinocytes","Dermatofibroma is keratin-filled and lipoma is fluid-filled","Dermatofibroma is a benign overgrowth of fibrous tissue in the dermis; lipoma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is a benign overgrowth of fibrous tissue in the dermis, and lipoma is a benign enclosed capsule of adipocytes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the two connective tissue layers of the dermis?","choices":["Basale and spinosum","Corneum and lucidum","The papillary and the subcutaneous, being the superficial and the deepest layers","Papillary, the thin superficial layer, and reticular, the dense deeper layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The dermis consists of two connective tissue layers: the thin superficial papillary layer and the dense deeper reticular layer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What defines a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["Any lesion that arises from malignant rather than from benign underlying disease processes","A modification or change of a primary lesion caused by infection, trauma or other factors","Any lesion appearing on a second body site after the first","Any lesion that is palpable rather than flat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble what it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which description of petechiae is accurate?","choices":["Mast cell histamine release with plasma leakage, up to 5 millimeters","Minor bleeding into the dermis from capillary rupture, infection, thrombocytopenia or vasculitis, 1 to 2 millimeters or less","Permanent dilatation of the superficial dermal vessels, measuring 1 to 2 millimeters across the surface of the skin","Increased melanin production in the basal layer, up to 5 millimeters"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Petechiae are non-blanchable pinpoint deposits of blood in the dermis, 1 to 2 millimeters or less, from minor bleeding such as capillary rupture. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which mechanism underlies basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["The Hedgehog signaling pathway, following ultraviolet-induced mutation of basal keratinocytes in the epidermis","The tp53 tumor suppressor pathway, acting in the differentiated keratinocytes of the epidermis","Fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, in immature keratinocytes","Uninhibited melanocyte growth at the dermal-epidermal junction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ultraviolet-induced mutation of basal keratinocytes overactivates Hedgehog signaling and drives tumor growth in this slow-growing, rarely metastasizing cancer. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which description of actinic keratosis is accurate?","choices":["Benign proliferation of immature keratinocytes, carrying a higher risk for basal cell carcinoma of the head and neck region in later life especially","Human papillomavirus infection of basal cells, carrying higher risk for melanoma","Autoimmune T cell infiltration, carrying higher risk for lymphoma","Damage from excessive cumulative ultraviolet exposure leading to intraepidermal proliferation of dysplastic keratinocytes, carrying higher risk for squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratosis is the most common precancer, arising on skin damaged by chronic ultraviolet exposure, and it carries a higher risk of squamous cell carcinoma. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How do petechiae, purpura and ecchymoses differ?","choices":["By size — petechiae 1 to 2 millimeters or less, purpura 3 to 10 millimeters, ecchymoses greater than 10 millimeters — with similar underlying pathology","By mechanism — petechiae from capillary rupture, purpura from vessel dilatation, and ecchymoses from melanin deposition in the dermis rather than blood","By depth — petechiae epidermal, purpura dermal, ecchymoses subcutaneous","By blanchability — petechiae blanch, purpura and ecchymoses do not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All three are bleeding into the dermis with similar underlying pathology, separated by size, from pinpoint petechiae to ecchymoses over 10 millimeters. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary from a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["A primary lesion is the one that appears first in time, and a secondary lesion is any lesion that appears later at a different site elsewhere on the body","A primary lesion is the direct result of the underlying disease and retains its original unmodified appearance; a secondary lesion has been modified over time","A primary lesion is palpable and a secondary lesion is flat","A primary lesion is benign and a secondary lesion is malignant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble the lesion it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which description of a fissure is accurate?","choices":["A thickened plaque produced by chronic rubbing","A dilated superficial vessel without inflammatory infiltrate","A linear ulcer forming a crack in the epidermis, from loss of elasticity or flexibility, severe dryness and mechanical tension, with hyperkeratosis thickening the stratum corneum","A superficial loss of the epidermis that spares the dermal-epidermal junction and everything beneath it, so no collagen is ever destroyed in the process of healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A fissure is a linear ulcer cracking the epidermis where lost elasticity, severe dryness and mechanical tension act on a thickened stratum corneum. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which tinea subtype affects the nails?","choices":["Tinea barbae, affecting nails and beard","Tinea unguium, also called onychomycosis","Tinea capitis, also called onychomycosis","Tinea manuum, affecting the nails of the hand"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corporis is trunk and limbs, pedis foot, cruris groin, capitis head, facialis face, manuum hand, barbae beard. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What tissue makes up a scale?","choices":["Focal loss of epidermis and dermis with destruction of the collagen beneath","A compact portion of desquamating stratum corneum, varying in size and thickness","Dried sebum, cellular debris, blood or necrotic skin","Thickened epidermis induced by long-term scratching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A scale is a compact portion of desquamating stratum corneum that varies in size and thickness, and psoriasis is an example. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where does each step of vitamin D activation take place, beginning in the skin?","choices":["Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin under ultraviolet B light, then hydroxylated first in the liver and then in the kidneys to the active form calcitriol","Calcitriol is produced directly in the skin under ultraviolet B light and requires no further modification in either the liver or the kidneys at all","Cholecalciferol is produced in the liver, then hydroxylated in the skin and finally in the kidneys","Cholecalciferol is produced in the skin and hydroxylated entirely within the kidneys in a single step"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The skin only starts the process; two further hydroxylation steps in separate organs are needed before the hormone is active. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which structural epidermal changes occur in psoriasis?","choices":["Separation of the epidermis from the dermis with fluid accumulating between the two layers of the skin itself","Loss of melanocytes from the basal layer","Epidermal hyperplasia, loss of the stratum granulosum, and failure of epidermal cells to secrete lipids producing xeroderma","Thinning of the epidermis with slowed keratinocyte division"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Active T cells drive keratinocyte proliferation into epidermal hyperplasia, the stratum granulosum is lost, and epidermal cells stop secreting lipids. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What defines telangiectasia at the tissue level?","choices":["Dense inflammatory infiltration around dilated vessels","Proliferation of new vessels driven by release of vascular endothelial growth factor into the surrounding dermis over a prolonged period","Permanent dilatation and thinning of the endothelium of capillaries, venules and arterioles in the superficial dermis, with NO inflammatory cell infiltration","Capillary rupture with blood deposited into the dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The absence of inflammatory infiltrate is the discriminating feature. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is sebum primarily composed of?","choices":["Water, sodium and chloride electrolytes","Keratin and keratohyalin","Collagen and elastin","Triglycerides, wax esters and squalene"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, produced by the sebaceous glands associated with hair follicles to lubricate skin and hair. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which layer of the epidermis is the site of cellular division?","choices":["The granular layer, known as the stratum granulosum","The stratum lucidum","The basal layer, or stratum basale, the deepest layer","The stratum corneum, the outermost layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A single layer of rapidly dividing columnar keratinocytes, which also contains melanocytes and Merkel cells. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which description of collagen in the remodeling phase of wound healing is accurate?","choices":["Type III collagen is replaced with stronger type I collagen oriented in small parallel bundles, whereas normal dermis has a basket-weave orientation","Type I collagen is replaced with weaker type III collagen oriented in a basket-weave pattern throughout the scar tissue that results","Collagen is degraded entirely and replaced by elastin","Collagen production continues to increase indefinitely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In remodeling, from day 21 to one year, type III collagen gives way to stronger type I in small parallel bundles, unlike the basket-weave of normal dermis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes androgen regulation of hair?","choices":["All three types are equally androgen-dependent","Terminal hair is androgen-regulated; vellus hair grows independently of androgens","Vellus hair is regulated by androgens; terminal hair grows independently of them","Lanugo hair is androgen-regulated; terminal hair is not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Thick terminal hair on the head, beard, axillae and pubic areas is androgen-regulated; fine vellus hair grows independently of androgens. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does human papillomavirus produce a common wart?","choices":["It triggers mast cell degranulation in the dermis","It digests keratin using keratinases and proteases","It invades epidermal basal cells through microabrasions, leading to proliferation of the epidermis","It infects the melanocytes that lie along the dermal-epidermal junction within the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Human papillomavirus invades epidermal basal cells through microabrasions, and that infection leads to proliferation of the epidermis. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which description of squamous cell carcinoma is accurate?","choices":["Overactivation of the Hedgehog pathway, with pearly papules and telangiectasias","Activating mutation of fibroblast growth factor receptor-3, with a stuck-on waxy and scaly appearance on the skin","Autosomal dominant inheritance with variable penetrance and radial growth","Mutation in the tp53 tumor suppressor gene, with keratin pearls and epithelial pearls as pathognomonic features"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma involves mutation of the tp53 tumor suppressor gene in keratinocytes, and keratin and epithelial pearls are pathognomonic of it. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why does psoriatic skin become dry?","choices":["The stratum corneum is lost entirely","Sebaceous glands overproduce sebum, which dries the surface","Epidermal cells fail to secrete lipids, producing xeroderma","Eccrine sweat glands are destroyed by inflammation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In psoriasis, epidermal cells do not secrete lipids, and that failure of secretion produces xeroderma, or dry skin. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"}]};
